{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "concern_category",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.97,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Directly answers the query by grouping visits into clinical concern types, giving a strong axis to aggregate and compare. Categories are well-grounded in the reason_for_visit text (e.g. knee pain->Musculoskeletal, MI follow-up->Cardiovascular). One borderline case: an annual physical with fatigue/BP/BMI was labeled 'Endocrine / Metabolic' when it is arguably preventive/general, a defensible but slightly overconfident reading, so groundedness is not perfect."
    },
    {
      "name": "chief_complaint",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.55,
      "source_groundedness": 0.97,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Extracts the leading clause of reason_for_visit as a concise chief complaint. Values are faithful, near-verbatim extractions from the source. However for the query about MAIN TYPES of concerns it is largely a truncation of an existing column and highly high-cardinality (mostly singletons), so it offers limited grouping leverage beyond concern_category, though it still aids drill-down/verification."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.82,
  "source_groundedness": 0.93,
  "rationale": "concern_category is a genuinely useful, well-grounded categorical axis that directly serves the 'main types of concerns' query. chief_complaint is faithfully extracted but mostly a restatement/truncation of reason_for_visit with high cardinality, limiting its analytical leverage for typing concerns. Both columns are soundly derivable from the source text with no invented facts; only minor overconfident categorisation (the physical-as-Endocrine case) slightly lowers groundedness."
}

